# Trivy gate baseline — pre-existing OS-package findings in the base images.
#
# ── What this file is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It is not a waiver, and it hides nothing. scripts/scan-image.sh runs Trivy
# twice and applies this file to ONE of the two passes:
#
#   report pass  --ignorefile /dev/null   every severity, every package type,
#                                         including everything listed here →
#                                         SARIF → GitHub code scanning.
#
#   gate pass    --ignorefile .trivyignore  fixable HIGH/CRITICAL OS packages,
#                                           minus this list → blocks the merge.
#
# The effect is that the gate blocks *regressions*. A newly disclosed CVE, or
# one a PR introduces via a base-image change, goes red on that PR. Debt that
# arrived with a third-party base image Atlas does not build stays visible in
# code scanning without pinning every unrelated PR red.
#
# ── Why there is a baseline at all ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every entry below was present in an upstream base image on the day the gate
# landed (#4822), and none was fixable by bumping a pin: the pinned digest for
# oven/bun:1.3.13 IS the current digest for that tag, so upstream has simply not
# rebuilt with the patched packages. "Fixable" in Trivy's sense means the distro
# published a fixed package version — not that a fixed base image exists for us
# to move to.
#
# Clearing them needs a change to shipped artifacts: an apt/apk upgrade in the
# runner stages, a rebase onto a refreshed base, or waiting for upstream to
# rebuild. That is deliberate product surface, and it was out of scope for the
# PR that added the gate, which landed during the 2026-07-27 ship freeze.
#
# ── Scope note ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only OS packages appear here, because only OS packages are gated. Library
# findings inside the images (npm, Go modules) are reported to code scanning but
# never block — they are Dependabot's remit, and it can open the fix PR. See the
# SCOPE section in scripts/scan-image.sh.
#
# ── How to clear it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Re-scan after any base-image change and delete every line that no longer
# appears. The expiry dates are the forcing function: once passed, Trivy stops
# honouring the entry and the gate goes red until it is fixed or consciously
# re-dated. Do not bulk-extend the dates — re-scan first.
#
#   bash scripts/list-runtime-base-images.sh | while read -r i; do \
#     trivy image --scanners vuln --pkg-types os --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#       --ignore-unfixed --ignorefile /dev/null "$i"; done
#
# Entries are global rather than per-image on purpose: the two runtime bases
# share 7 of these, so per-image files would duplicate them and drift apart.
#
# Baseline captured 2026-07-27. Expiry 2026-09-01 — after the v0.2.0 release on
# 2026-08-03, with slack to schedule the base-image work.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared by BOTH runtime bases (oven/bun:1.3.13 and caddy:2.10-alpine).
# openssl plus libcap — the same upstream advisories reaching Debian and Alpine.
# These ship in every Atlas image, including the create-atlas docker templates.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HIGH     libcrypto3 (debian: libssl3t64)
CVE-2026-28387 exp:2026-09-01
# HIGH     libcrypto3 (debian: libssl3t64)
CVE-2026-28388 exp:2026-09-01
# HIGH     libcrypto3 (debian: libssl3t64)
CVE-2026-28389 exp:2026-09-01
# HIGH     libcrypto3 (debian: libssl3t64)
CVE-2026-28390 exp:2026-09-01
# CRITICAL libcrypto3 (debian: libssl3t64)
CVE-2026-31789 exp:2026-09-01
# HIGH     libcrypto3 (debian: libssl3t64)
CVE-2026-45447 exp:2026-09-01
# HIGH     libcap (debian: libcap2)
CVE-2026-4878 exp:2026-09-01

